questionAnswers.Q1:
Faithful. The diff of v1 and v2 is exactly what the README discloses:
- the base is i05_gate_channel_v2.Gate and the fence is Gate.fence();
- the drain is the I05 v2 drain, and denies are computed with _denied_since;
- a pending drain is also settled after writer events;
- maintenance is ENDED in every rebuilt generation;
- the sealed-kind classification is new;
- request_digest is factored out and gives the same digest as v1;
- the upstream outcome is split out (pending, _w_upstream, _abort_pending, LOCKSTEP);
- observed fields are null on rejections.
Maintenance follows fence() after every event and never uses the drain answer. OPEN->HELD is implemented (W02b-F V2), and HELD never reopens. Every v1 property the v1 review confirmed held in the fuzz oracle, and nothing is weakened. One behaviour change is disclosed: a clean restart (T47) now also ends the maintenance.

questionAnswers.Q2:
No. No interleaving I found violates any listed property:
- a randomized single-process campaign of 6,000 runs and 280,000 events (2,303 MAINT_OPEN, 656 forwarded writes);
- a failpoint campaign: single, adjacent and gap-2 journal failures, torn and untorn, with and without a marker failure, at every record ordinal of all 58 transcripts (5,232 runs);
- 0 oracle violations and 0 exceptions.
The oracle checked, independently of the model:
- at every WRITE_FORWARDED: maintenance and fence() OPEN, gate not HELD, no restart in the run, failure marker empty, no torn record, owner session, and every CONSUMED since BIND with a durable TERMINAL or SEALED record (journal-derived);
- no MAINT_REQUESTED or MAINT_OPEN after a restart, and no CONSUMED after MAINT_REQUESTED;
- at WRITER_SESSION_ENDED and MAINT_ENDED: no pending write, and a WRITE_TERMINAL attempt for every WRITE_FORWARDED;
- no relay after an abort or of a non-pending write;
- classification equal to the outcome rule, downgraded to IO_AMBIGUOUS on a failed WRITE_TERMINAL, and DELIVERED_RESULT only with a durable WRITE_TERMINAL DELIVERED_RESULT;
- maintenance OPEN/HELD/PENDING equal to fence() after every event, with no reopen;
- the I05 v2 invariant (drainPending only in DRAINING or INVALIDATED with an action in flight);
- an ambiguous write implies HELD, and the state is never ACTIVE after MAINT_REQUESTED.
The model's own check_frame validated every I05 and I07 response it produced. Four seeded mutants were each caught: abort as a no-op, no OPEN->HELD transition, maintenance in a rebuilt generation, and a write while PENDING.

questionAnswers.Q3:
Yes. Independently recomputing planeon:policy-writer's POLICY_WRITE grants from identity-closure.json (sha256 0a9b82ff..., equal to the pin) gives 9 (resource, scope) rows:
- the 7 writable rows match, with operations plus unusedVerbs equal to the granted verbs and only patch unused;
- the 2 admission rows are exactly the sealed notWritable entries (closureResource and closureScope match);
- bind and escalate stay SPECIAL.
The sealed kinds return ADMISSION_OBJECT_SEALED for all 216 probed combinations: versions v1, v1beta1, v1alpha1, v1alpha2, v2 and v9; every operation; namespace null, qual or other; object null or set. The kind step runs before the verb, scope, prior and object rules (K49 and K63 were SCOPE_MISMATCH and OBJECT_RULE in v1). Only the maintenance and writeId checks come earlier, as in v1. The other admission kinds stay POLICY_KIND_NOT_WRITABLE.

reviewLimit:
This is a read-only, source-level review by an agent separate from the author. I edited no repository file. Probe scripts exist only under /private/tmp/claude-501/w02cf-review-r1/. I ran no pytest, scripts/validate_*.py, runner, native, cloud, network or GitHub action.

The conclusions hold within the reference model's abstraction:
- in-memory journal and marker with one-shot injected failures;
- writer custody as an event;
- the upstream exchange as a W_UPSTREAM event, with no clock, so the 10-second bound is not modelled;
- one generation;
- no real socket, TLS, HTTP, fsync or apiserver.
The randomized and failpoint campaigns are bounded, not exhaustive. Kubernetes semantics were not re-checked against a pinned upstream tag.

The REVIEW_BRIEF.md digest was taken from source-index.json and not recomputed, because of the coordinator's PAUSE. Repository cleanliness (git status) was not re-checked after the runs for the same reason. All runs used PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 and an external UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT, and wrote only to the scratch directory.

This PASS is not an installed gate or writer, enrollment (W03), the W02f guard's installation or any E01-E12 proof. W1 (MINOR) and W2-W4 (NOTE) may be carried, but each must be fixed or explicitly carried in the W02c-F status record.
